+# The Daily Grind
+
+This is a static page. Load `coffee-card.js` as a browser module to register the
+autonomous `<coffee-card>` element. The existing cards retain light DOM fallback
+content so their details and links remain usable if JavaScript does not load.
+After upgrade, each card renders its attributes into an open shadow root.
+
+```html
+<coffee-card name="Maple Oat Latte" description="Maple syrup and oat milk."
+  href="/menu/seasonal" price="4.75" featured></coffee-card>
+```
+
+| Attribute / property | Type | Default / behavior |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `name` | string | Empty; rendered as the heading. |
+| `description` | string | Empty; rendered as plain text. |
+| `href` | string | Empty; relative and HTTP(S) links are enabled. |
+| `price` | number | `0`; shown in USD only when the attribute is present. Negative or nonfinite values read as `0`. |
+| `featured` | boolean | `false`; attribute presence displays a Featured badge. |
+
+Properties reflect to attributes, and attribute changes immediately update the
+properties and rendered nodes. Set a string property to `null` or `undefined` to
+remove its attribute. Remove `price` to hide the price. Set `card.featured = false`
+or remove the attribute to clear the badge; `featured="false"` means **true**,
+following native HTML boolean semantics. Properties assigned before registration
+are replayed on connection.
+
+```js
+const card = document.querySelector('coffee-card');
+card.name = 'Maple Oat Latte';
+card.price = 4.75;
+card.featured = true;
+card.addEventListener('coffee-select', ({ detail }) => console.log(detail));
+```
+
+Activating the native details link emits a bubbling, composed `coffee-select`
+event with `{ name, href, price, featured }` while retaining normal navigation.
+Connection attaches the listener idempotently; disconnection removes it, and
+reconnection restores it. Updates retain existing shadow nodes and focus.
+Page theme variables (`--primary`, `--accent`, `--text`) inherit into each card.
+
+Run the DOM unit checks with `pnpm test`. These use Happy DOM in Node, without a
+browser or a server. No build step is required.
