@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static homepage with a browser-to-equipment connection panel. No backend or build step is required.
+
+## Equipment connections
+
+The panel sends a GET request directly from the visitor’s browser to the configured status URL, only after they select Connect. It does not discover devices, relay requests through a server, start brewing, or persist addresses. Each card works independently.
+
+Default endpoints (change them in the panel to match your equipment):
+
+- Brewer: `http://192.168.1.100/api/status`, on the visitor’s private Wi-Fi network.
+- Desktop helper: `http://127.0.0.1:8765/api/status`, on the computer running the browser. `localhost` and `[::1]` also work.
+
+The endpoint must return a successful HTTP status (for example, `200 OK`). No response body format is assumed. “Connected” means the last status check succeeded, not a persistent transport connection. Disconnecting resets the card; reconnect to check availability again. Requests time out after 12 seconds and can be cancelled.
+
+Serve the homepage over HTTPS (localhost is also a secure context). Supporting browsers can prompt for local network access. Requests set `targetAddressSpace` separately to `local` for the brewer and `loopback` for the helper. Browser support and site/OS network permissions can still prevent connections; the panel reports failures and offers troubleshooting. See the [Chrome Local Network Access guide](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/local-network-access).
+
+Both equipment endpoints need CORS support. For a homepage hosted at `https://coffee.example`, allow that exact origin:
+
+```http
+Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://coffee.example
+Vary: Origin
+```
+
+Requests omit credentials and reject redirects. Configure the equipment’s status endpoint for direct access without cookie authentication. If your equipment/browser requires an OPTIONS preflight, handle it with the allowed website origin and GET method; older Private Network Access implementations may additionally require `Access-Control-Allow-Private-Network: true`. Browser permission does not replace CORS. An inaccessible or blocked response will never be treated as a successful connection.
+
+## Checks
+
+Run `node --check equipment.js` and `node --test tests/equipment.test.cjs`. These checks use mocked requests and do not start a server or access real equipment.
