 The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.

+## Developer diagnostics widget
+
+The homepage has independent browser connections for a hardware test device on a private Wi-Fi LAN and a companion CLI on loopback. Replace the example URLs with your service's read-only HTTP(S) health endpoint, including port and path. No companion CLI is bundled with this news app.
+
+The endpoint should return a successful HTTP status and an optional text or JSON diagnostics body. It must allow the deployed page's exact origin with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (an origin has no `/devtools-times/` path). No cookies or authorization headers are sent. Requests go directly from the reader's browser, never through the application server, and start only after Connect is selected. Redirects are rejected.
+
+Serve the news app over HTTPS. Supporting browsers ask separately for `local-network` and `loopback-network` access; denied permissions can be changed in browser site settings. Unsupported permission queries fall back to a manual connection attempt. If the browser blocks HTTP endpoints, use a browser with Local Network Access support or a trusted HTTPS endpoint. Standard CORS remains required; deprecated Private Network Access preflight headers are not used. See [Chrome's Local Network Access guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/local-network-access).
+
+Connections are one-shot reachability checks, with a 20-second timeout and a cancel button. Up to 4,000 response characters can be expanded after a successful check. The LAN form accepts private IPv4 ranges and `.local` names; the CLI form accepts loopback IPv4, `localhost` names, and `[::1]`.
+
+Run endpoint validation and request configuration checks with `node --test tests/diagnostics.test.mjs` (Node 24+).
+
