--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# The Daily Grind account UI
+
+The static entry page is the My Account / Sign-in & security view. The original coffee homepage remains at `home.html`. No server or dependencies are required for the page assets.
+
+## Preview and production data
+
+With no configuration, the UI explicitly shows a preview account with three sample passkeys. Rename and delete changes persist in localStorage, including deleting the last key. Preview records never reach the browser authenticator and are not authentication credentials.
+
+This repository has no authentication service, database, or passkey backend. To connect an existing signed-in customer backend, add these tags to the document head before the module script:
+
+```html
+<meta name="passkey-api" content="/api">
+<meta name="csrf-token" content="SERVER_GENERATED_SESSION_CSRF_TOKEN">
+```
+
+The session-authenticated endpoints must implement:
+
+- `GET /api/credentials`: `{ "rpId": "YOUR_REGISTRATION_RP_ID", "userId": "BASE64URL_USER_HANDLE", "credentials": [...] }`. Return the **complete**, unpaginated credential list belonging to the authenticated customer. Credential records contain `id` (unpadded Base64URL), `name`, `aaguid`, `registeredAt` (epoch milliseconds), and nullable `lastUsedAt` (epoch milliseconds). Optional `device` values: `laptop`, `phone`, or `key`.
+- `PUT /api/credential/:id`: accept `{ "name": "Nickname" }`, validate 1–60 characters, enforce ownership, persist, and return JSON or HTTP 204.
+- `DELETE /api/credential/:id`: enforce ownership, remove the credential from the authentication database, and return JSON or HTTP 204.
+
+Require a valid signed-in session and CSRF validation for mutations. The UI sends session cookies and the `X-CSRF-Token` header. The server must never trust a client-supplied customer identity. Before allowing removal of the final passkey, ensure the customer retains another usable sign-in method (or return an actionable error). Use HTTPS in production.
+
+After initial loading and successful deletion, the UI feature-detects `PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials` and sends only a fresh, complete server snapshot with the server's configured RP ID and Base64URL IDs. Empty lists are intentionally synchronized. Unsupported browsers and failed synchronization leave account management usable and display manual password-manager cleanup guidance. A signal is a notification; the authenticator controls whether to apply it. Nickname changes do not invoke `signalCurrentUserDetails`, since they do not change the account identity.
+
+The UI has no passkey registration or authentication ceremony; those require a separately verified WebAuthn backend.
+
+## Checks
+
+`npm test` runs Node tests for persistence, validation, authenticated API requests, and authenticator synchronization. `node --check account.js` checks the UI script without running it. No build step is required.
