@@ -15,3 +15,31 @@ The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Account settings and passkeys
+
+Signed-in readers can open **Account settings** from the header to register,
+rename, or remove passkeys. Password sign-in is verified by the existing Times
+login service; the app then creates a server session. Passkeys can also sign in
+through the verified WebAuthn flow. No passwords or bearer tokens are stored in
+the browser.
+
+This feature requires Node.js 22.13+ (Node.js 24 recommended). Credential public
+keys and account handles are stored in SQLite at `.data/accounts.sqlite`.
+Set `ACCOUNT_DB_PATH` to a file on a persistent volume for deployments. The
+SQLite database and Astro's filesystem session storage must persist and be
+shared appropriately when deploying more than one app instance.
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS (or localhost). Set `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN` to the public origin
+(e.g. `https://news.example.com`) when deploying behind a proxy; its hostname is
+the relying-party ID. Keep the origin stable so registered passkeys remain usable.
+
+The browser receives a complete authenticated credential snapshot through the
+[WebAuthn Signal API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PublicKeyCredential/signalAllAcceptedCredentials_static)
+after loading or changing passkeys. When synchronization is unavailable or
+fails, the account change remains saved and the page shows retry/manual cleanup
+guidance. Passkey display names are account-local nicknames, not authenticator
+account identities.
+
+Run `pnpm test` for storage isolation, session/origin guards, validation, and
+browser synchronization tests. Run `pnpm build` to compile the application.
