+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive account experience with passkey registration, discoverable sign-in, passkey naming and removal, profile updates, and step-up verification. The initial screen is a clearly labeled, locally saved **preview account**. Preview credentials never authenticate and no preview operation changes server accounts. Select **Create yours** or sign out to use real accounts.
+
+## Commands
+
+- `pnpm install`
+- `pnpm build` — build the client
+- `pnpm check` — syntax checks without importing the server
+- `pnpm test` — isolated account rules and SQLite persistence tests; no server
+- `pnpm start` — build and serve on port 11222 (or `PORT`)
+
+## Configuration
+
+Passkeys work on HTTPS or localhost. Set `APP_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin (e.g. `https://coffee.example.com`) and optionally `RP_ID` to its registrable parent domain. Defaults are `http://localhost:11222` (using `PORT` when set) and that origin’s hostname. Origin and RP identity are fixed configuration and are never inferred from untrusted request headers. An HTTPS reverse proxy can forward to the Node process. Secure cookies are enabled whenever the configured origin is HTTPS.
+
+`DATABASE_PATH` defaults to `./data/accounts.sqlite`. Back up this file; it contains user profiles and passkey public keys, never private keys or biometric data. Sessions are held in memory, expire after eight hours, and are invalidated by restart. A multi-instance deployment needs a shared session store and shared rate limiting.
+
+Native WebAuthn browser calls are verified with SimpleWebAuthn on the server. Discoverable credentials support username-free login; step-up authentication requires user verification and restricts the credential list to the active account. Challenges expire in five minutes, are bound to the session and ceremony, and are single use. Cookies are HttpOnly and SameSite Strict. Mutation endpoints require the configured Origin. Profile changes, new passkeys for existing accounts, and passkey removals require fresh verification. The final passkey cannot be removed. Browser Signal APIs synchronize accepted credentials and updated profiles when available; the client uses a bundled WebAuthn polyfill for JSON and capability APIs.
+
+Email is an account identifier, not a verified email or recovery method. There is no email delivery or recovery flow; users should add a backup passkey. Coffee ordering, shop addresses, rewards fulfillment, and support email delivery are outside this account implementation. The surrounding store dialogs are informational.
+
+Validation for this change deliberately uses build, syntax, and isolated tests only. No development server or browser verification is required.
