@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+# Privacy implementation and audit
+
+The current application is a static brochure site. No forms, client-side scripts,
+analytics, cookies, browser storage, permission requests, or application logs are
+needed. Keep those defaults; do not collect personal data speculatively. The
+privacy notice at `/privacy-policy/` describes the scope and hosting limitations.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Resource | Before | Current handling |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Hero photo | Runtime request to `images.unsplash.com`, exposing visitor connection metadata | Existing stock photo downloaded to `assets/coffee.jpg`; served locally |
+| Fonts | System fonts | No external font provider |
+| Scripts, analytics, embeds, APIs | None | None; blocked by CSP |
+| Hosting | Receives page and asset requests | Operator must configure access-log minimization and retention |
+| Development tooling | `statikk` via `pnpm dlx` | Development only; never included in the production build |
+
+Photo source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085
+
+Run `npm run audit:privacy` before release and after any dependency, asset, script,
+or integration change. The source audit rejects external resource URLs, client
+scripts, embeds, forms, inline handlers, and several other tracking surfaces;
+checks local resource files; and checks that HTML CSP fallbacks match the headers.
+It is a source check, not proof of the deployed host's behavior. Re-review any
+new public directories and update the explicit build and audit lists together.
+
+Before introducing a third party, document its purpose, request destinations,
+data fields, retention, and deletion mechanism. Prefer local assets and static
+links. Optional embeds must use a local facade and load only after an informed
+user action. Never send account or checkout information to analytics providers.
+Do not add fingerprinting or high-entropy device collection. Any future script
+requires deliberate CSP changes and a review for unsafe DOM sinks and storage.
+
+## Deployment
+
+`npm run build` audits the site and copies only public files to `dist/`.
+`netlify.toml` configures Netlify to deploy that folder; `dist/_headers` applies
+the HTTP policies to every route. Other hosts must configure the equivalent
+response headers themselves. The existing `start` command is only a static
+development convenience and is not configured to enforce these headers.
+
+HTML provides a CSP and referrer fallback before loading resources. Frame
+ancestors, Permissions-Policy, HSTS, and other response-only protections require
+the host's HTTP headers; meta tags cannot replace them. Scripts, outbound API
+connections, forms, workers, and frames are disabled. Local CSS and images are
+allowed. Trusted Types blocks DOM injection sinks in supporting browsers.
+
+Require HTTPS and redirect HTTP at the hosting edge. HSTS starts at 300 seconds;
+increase it after validating the production HTTPS setup. Subdomains and preload
+are deliberately excluded until their HTTPS coverage is known. No reporting
+endpoint is configured: this repository has no backend to receive and redact
+reports, and sending raw violation URLs or script samples could collect secrets.
+No experimental connection header is required for this script-free site.
+
+Configure the hosting provider to omit URL query strings, cookies, authorization
+headers, and request bodies from logs. Retain only operationally necessary
+metadata for a documented short period, then delete it automatically. Confirm
+the provider's retention and access controls before making stronger public claims.
+
+## Requirements before adding accounts or orders
+
+The existing account/order/logout URLs have no handlers in this repository.
+Client-side cleanup or a static header rule cannot invalidate a server session.
+When implementing those services:
+
+- Collect only fields needed for the immediate transaction; support guest orders.
+  Explain each field's purpose and retention alongside the input. Separate
+  optional marketing consent; never repurpose transaction data silently.
+- Keep PII and tokens out of URLs, client storage, analytics, logs, and errors.
+  Use safe DOM APIs such as `textContent` instead of inserting untrusted HTML.
+- Validate and authorize on the server, rate-limit sensitive endpoints, encrypt
+  transport and stored sensitive records, and limit access to necessary staff.
+- Use a server-issued `__Host-session` cookie with `Secure; HttpOnly;
+  SameSite=Lax; Path=/` and no Domain attribute. Do not store tokens in JavaScript.
+- Implement logout as a CSRF-protected state-changing request. Revoke the session,
+  expire its cookie, and return `Cache-Control: no-store` and
+  `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"` on its successful response.
+  Do not apply Clear-Site-Data to ordinary pages. Mark authenticated responses
+  `Cache-Control: no-store` and prevent service workers from caching them.
+- Provide accessible data access, correction, and deletion controls. Define and
+  automate retention/deletion, including backups, before collecting data.
