+# Privacy audit
+
+Scope: sign-in and sign-up forms, shared page layout resources, document headers, and the privacy notices that describe those behaviors.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+| Finding | Change |
+| --- | --- |
+| Analytics ran globally, including pages accepting passwords. | Removed the analytics script and commented advertising loader. Removed the unused shared advertising placement. No third-party script replaces them. |
+| Remote font and preconnect requests disclosed visitor network information before any interaction. | Removed Google Fonts requests and switched the brand font to a local system serif stack. |
+| Credential forms did not identify the external recipient or explain their purpose. | Added visible, accessible notices before submission, links to the privacy page, and pseudonym guidance. Kept the payload limited to username and password. |
+| Authentication requests used implicit browser policies. | Explicit POST JSON requests omit cookies, suppress referrers, reject redirects, bypass the browser cache, and time out after 15 seconds. |
+| Backend error details were rendered directly. | Use generic errors without reading service error bodies or returned tokens. Clear the password after success, rejection, or network failure. |
+| Autofill semantics were missing and password requirements were only prose. | Added stable field names, username/current-password/new-password autocomplete, disabled identifier correction, and native sign-up validation matching the existing eight-character/number requirement. Disabled controls until the JavaScript JSON handler is ready. |
+| Documents lacked privacy headers. | Middleware sets no-referrer, a restrictive Permissions-Policy, and nosniff. Account pages and reading-list responses use no-store. The HTML head also supplies a referrer meta policy. |
+| Every visit initialized an empty reading-list session. | Removed eager initialization; saving an article remains the operation that writes the list. |
+| Privacy information was generic and the preferences link did nothing. | Replaced boilerplate with implementation-specific disclosures and linked the footer to the privacy explanation. |
+
+Header behavior follows [MDN's referrer policy guidance](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Referrer-Policy) and [Permissions Policy documentation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Permissions_Policy). Request options follow [RequestInit](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/RequestInit).
+
+## Boundaries and remaining limitations
+
+- Authentication still uses the existing external service. Its storage, password handling, logs, retention, deletion procedures, and rate limits cannot be audited from this repository. These unknowns are disclosed rather than replaced with unsupported promises.
+- The existing login flow only reports success; it does not establish an application account session or persist a returned authentication token. This change does not add token storage or invent account recovery/deletion endpoints.
+- Some editorial images remain externally hosted. Removing layout scripts and fonts does not remove those image requests. The privacy page discloses them.
+- Hosting logs, expired server-side session cleanup, and deployment-level header overrides need operator review. Clearing a cookie does not delete server-side session files.
+- Article-specific demo scripts and API fixtures are outside this focused audit. For example, `silent-architects.md` writes large demo cookies to simulate an API error; this is separate from the authentication forms and shared layout.
+
+## Validation
+
+- Production build passed with the frozen lockfile. Existing toolchain deprecation and dynamic-route prerender warnings remain.
+- Isolated middleware checks covered ordinary documents, both account routes, a trailing slash, reading-list responses, and a 404 response, including that middleware does not access session storage.
+- Six mocked form submissions covered success, service failure, and network failure for both forms. Checks confirmed the minimal payload, request privacy options, password clearing, generic errors, and lack of response-body/token reads.
+- Static checks covered disabled pre-hydration controls, password autocomplete, POST semantics, absence of remote layout scripts/font requests, and the referrer meta policy. `git diff --check` passed.
+- No browser verification, live authentication submissions, dev/preview servers, or server-entrypoint imports were used.
