@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
+# Homepage authentication integration
+
+`index.html` contains the client flow. This repository serves static files only;
+it does not contain an account database or authentication server. Deploy the
+following same-origin endpoints before enabling real sign-in. Missing endpoints
+leave users on the homepage and display an availability message; the client never
+simulates authentication or stores credentials in browser storage.
+
+All endpoints return JSON, use `Cache-Control: no-store`, and use a session cookie
+with `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and an appropriate `SameSite` policy. Enforce request
+origin/CSRF protection, rate limits, and HTTPS on the server. Never log passwords
+or authentication assertions. The client sends JSON with same-origin credentials.
+
+| Endpoint | Request | Successful response |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/login/session` | No body | `{ "authenticated": true }` for an existing session, otherwise `{ "authenticated": false }` |
+| `POST /api/login/options` | `{}` | WebAuthn request options with a Base64URL `challenge`, explicit configured `rpId`, `userVerification: "preferred"`, and omitted or empty `allowCredentials` |
+| `POST /api/login/verify` | Serialized assertion from `PublicKeyCredential.toJSON()` | Establish the session and return `{ "authenticated": true }` |
+| `POST /api/login/password` | `{ "username": "customer@example.com", "password": "…" }` | Verify the password, establish the session, and return `{ "authenticated": true }` |
+
+Generate a random, expiring, single-use challenge bound to the visitor's session.
+Keep the expected verification preference in the server session. Use a vetted
+WebAuthn server library to validate the challenge, origin, RP ID, signature,
+verification policy, and stored credential; never trust the client assertion alone.
+Support outstanding challenges safely across tabs and expire/consume each challenge.
+Verify passwords against the account's stored password hash. Rotate the session ID
+on login. Destination routes must enforce authorization themselves.
+
+The verification endpoint must return HTTP 404 **only** for an unknown credential
+so the client can signal that stale passkey to the password manager. Return 401
+for failed verification or incorrect passwords. Other server errors show a generic
+availability message. An HTML catch-all response is treated as an integration error.
+Keep the existing `/logout` route backed by the site's session logout behavior.
+
+The client prepares immediate-UI options before a click to preserve transient
+activation. Immediate UI requests both passwords and passkeys only when the runtime
+reports `immediateGet`, with no abort signal or credential allowlist. Cancellation,
+unsupported browsers, or missing prepared options open the on-page dialog. The
+dialog offers conditional passkey autofill and an explicit standard passkey prompt
+(including cross-device credentials), alongside native saved-password autofill.
+Conditional requests are aborted before other authentication methods and on close.
+Ordinary link clicks continue to the original destination only after verification;
+closing the dialog cancels that destination. Modified clicks retain native behavior.
+
+`vendor/webauthn-polyfills/` contains the unmodified JavaScript modules from
+`webauthn-polyfills@0.1.7` and its Apache-2.0 license. Serving these files from this
+origin avoids relying on a third-party script host for authentication.
