+# Daily Grind
+
+Requires Node.js 20 or newer. `npm start` serves the homepage and newsletter API
+on port 11222 (override with `PORT` and `HOST`). `npm run build` checks JavaScript
+syntax without starting a server. There are no runtime dependencies.
+
+Signups POST to `/api/newsletter` and are appended to `data/subscribers.jsonl`.
+Set `NEWSLETTER_FILE` to a persistent private path in production and back it up.
+Only the homepage, stylesheet and script are served; subscriber files are never
+public routes. This collects subscriptions; newsletter delivery is not configured.
+
+Deploy behind HTTPS and preserve the Node server's response headers. The enforced
+`Connection-Allowlist` permits only the response origin and
+`https://images.unsplash.com`; redirects and WebRTC remain blocked by default.
+CSP additionally limits connections and form submissions to this server, allows
+Unsplash only for images, and blocks injected scripts using per-response nonces.
+Styles are local files so inline-style permissions are unnecessary.
+
+Connection-Allowlist has limited browser support. CSP is the fallback, but cannot
+cover every channel (such as arbitrary top-level navigations and WebRTC) in browsers
+without Connection-Allowlist. An allowed host remains a trusted destination.
+Do not add third-party hosts without reviewing the data exposure.
+
+Both policies report to the same-origin `/api/security-reports` endpoint. It bounds
+and discards reports to avoid retaining customer URLs; connect a redacted monitoring
+pipeline here if needed. Failed, blocked and timed-out signup requests display a
+retry message while preserving the email field. Native form POST also works without
+JavaScript.
