+# Security audit and deployment
+
+The audit found one inline stylesheet, one inline style attribute, no JavaScript,
+no DOM injection sinks, and no existing security headers. The only external
+resource is the hero photograph at `https://images.unsplash.com`. There are no
+OAuth popups, embeds, APIs, webhooks, or browser features that need permissions.
+Existing navigation destinations still display the landing page; unknown paths
+now return 404 instead of silently serving HTML. Source files are never public.
+
+The CSS is now local and external. The enforced Content Security Policy allows
+only that stylesheet and the existing image origin, blocks all scripts and
+outbound script connections, disallows inline styles/event handlers, and prevents
+plugin content, base URL injection, framing, and cross-origin form submissions.
+Trusted Types blocks DOM injection sinks in supporting browsers, with no policy
+creation allowed. Other browsers retain the ordinary CSP protections. There is
+no client-side JavaScript to audit or authorize, so no nonce, script hash, SRI,
+or permissive Trusted Types shim is needed. If scripts are introduced, audit them
+and use explicit hashes or fresh per-response nonces before changing the policy.
+
+Every response carries MIME sniffing protection, a no-referrer policy, disabled
+unused browser permissions, no-store caching, same-origin resource protection,
+and COOP `same-origin-allow-popups`. Fetch Metadata rejects cross-origin resource
+requests while allowing top-level GET/HEAD navigation. No cross-origin integration
+or CORS grant is configured. COEP is deliberately omitted: this site needs no
+shared-memory isolation, and enabling it could break the external photograph.
+
+## Logout
+
+The navigation submits POST `/logout`. GET (including `/logout/`) displays a
+confirmation form and HEAD has no sign-out side effects. POST requires an exact
+Origin match to `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`, plus same-origin Fetch Metadata when supplied.
+This prevents cross-site logout and accidental sign-out from prefetching.
+
+Successful logout sends `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`, expires
+root-path `__Host-session` and `session` cookies, and returns an uncached signed-out
+page. The secure host-prefixed cookie expiry is appropriate for HTTPS; the legacy
+session expiry also supports local HTTP. No new session is created.
+
+There is no authentication provider, token issuer, or server-side session store
+in this repository. This implements browser-state sign-out for the current site.
+When authentication is added, revoke the server-side session/token in the POST
+handler before returning success and expire the exact cookie names, domain and
+path used by that provider. Browser cleanup alone cannot revoke a copied token.
+`Clear-Site-Data` requires HTTPS and browser support. Its cookies directive can
+also clear cookies across the registrable domain and subdomains; deploy with
+that scope understood. Avoid storing authentication tokens in Web Storage.
+
+## Deployment and policy rollout
+
+- Use Node 22 or newer. `npm start` runs the small dependency-free Node server.
+  `PORT` defaults to 11222. There is no build artifact or static-server dependency.
+- Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the actual visitor-facing origin, for example
+  `https://coffee.example`. It defaults to `http://localhost:PORT` for local use.
+  This is required for correct logout Origin checks and absolute reporting URLs.
+  Host and proxy headers are not trusted to choose security configuration.
+- Terminate HTTPS at the hosting platform/reverse proxy and restrict direct
+  access to the Node backend. HTTPS origins receive HSTS with a short 300-second
+  lifetime; raise it after validating TLS operation. Subdomain coverage and
+  preload are intentionally not enabled without a domain-wide audit.
+- CSP defaults to enforcement. For a future dependency change, use
+  `CSP_MODE=report-only` on staging or a controlled rollout. Baseline framing,
+  object, base and form protections stay enforced. Collect real traffic over
+  days/weeks and address legitimate violations before returning to
+  `CSP_MODE=enforce`. Do not fix violations by adding unsafe-inline or unsafe-eval.
+- Both modes advertise `Reporting-Endpoints` and CSP `report-to`, with legacy
+  `report-uri` fallback. POST `/security-reports` accepts the Reporting API and
+  legacy CSP JSON formats, limits bodies to 16 KiB, and logs at most 20 allowlisted
+  directive names per request. Raw reports, script samples, URLs, query strings,
+  cookies and referrers are never logged by the app. Filter low-volume extension
+  noise and investigate recurring directives against the source code.
+- Apply request rate limits to `/security-reports` at the edge, set log retention,
+  and disable request-body logging there. Avoid query strings in edge access
+  logs: application sanitization cannot erase data already logged by a proxy.
+  The collector is diagnostic, unauthenticated input, never an audit authority.
+
+## Checks
+
+`npm run build` performs syntax checks. `npm test` exercises the request handler
+using in-memory requests and responses: no server sockets, browser sessions,
+preview processes, or server entrypoint imports. Checks cover policy/markup
+compatibility, logout and CSRF, resource isolation, route restrictions, report
+limits and redaction, and staged rollout configuration.
