+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+The site has no authentication provider, session store, login flow, client-side
+JavaScript, third-party scripts, DOM injection sinks, or dynamic user content.
+Existing navigation routes still display the original landing page. No new
+authentication system is implied by the sign-out interface.
+
+## Changes
+
+- `npm start` runs a dependency-free Node HTTP server. Only known document routes
+  and `styles.css` are served; project files and unknown paths return 404.
+- The former logout response only displayed text. Logout now uses a same-origin
+  POST form. GET/HEAD `/logout` shows a confirmation without changing state, so
+  link previews, crawlers, and speculative fetches cannot sign a visitor out.
+  Missing, null, or foreign Origin headers fail closed; cross-site Fetch Metadata
+  is also rejected. Forwarded headers cannot override the configured origin.
+- Successful logout sends `Clear-Site-Data: "cache", "cookies", "storage"` and
+  expires root-path cookies received in the request, plus `session` and
+  `__Host-session`. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+- Inline CSS and the inline heading style were moved to `styles.css`. There are
+  no inline event handlers or scripts to exempt from CSP. Logout works without JS.
+- CSP denies resources by default, all scripts, plugins, frames, workers,
+  connections, base URL overrides and embedding. Styles are same-origin; images
+  allow only this origin and the existing Unsplash host. No `unsafe-inline`,
+  `unsafe-eval`, permissive Trusted Types policy, or additional external resource
+  is needed. Trusted Types enforcement adds protection in supporting browsers.
+- Responses include MIME sniffing protection, frame denial, a referrer policy,
+  restricted browser permissions, and same-origin opener/resource policies.
+  `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` preserves form Origin headers needed by the
+  logout check; `no-referrer` can turn them into `null`.
+
+## Deployment settings
+
+Keep `npm start` as the launch command. `PORT` defaults to `11222`.
+Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin, for example
+`https://coffee.example.com` (no trailing slash). This is required behind TLS
+termination so form Origin validation uses the browser-facing origin. The proxy
+should enforce the public host and terminate HTTPS. With an HTTPS public origin,
+the app sends HSTS and upgrades insecure subresource requests. Forwarded headers
+are deliberately ignored. Without a configured origin, direct HTTP operation
+compares Origin to the request Host and does not emit HSTS or HTTPS upgrades.
+
+`SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce` is the default and matches the current markup.
+For future resource changes, deploy first to staging with
+`SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only`. The full candidate policy then reports
+violations in browser developer tools while a baseline still enforces script,
+plugin, framing, base URL and form restrictions. There is no report collection
+endpoint or remote telemetry. Review violations through your deployment QA
+process, authorize only needed resources, and switch back to `enforce`. Report-only
+mode is not a substitute for enforcement. Do not add a permissive Trusted Types
+default policy to hide violations.
+
+## Session boundaries
+
+Clear-Site-Data depends on browser support and a secure context. Cookie clearing
+may affect sibling subdomains sharing a registrable domain; deploy this site on a
+dedicated domain if those sessions must be independent. Explicit cookie expiry is
+a fallback for host-only, root-path cookies; it cannot delete arbitrary cookies
+with different Domain/Path attributes or guarantee removal of every local store
+on unsupported browsers. The page does not collect or reflect cookie values.
+
+If authentication is added, invalidate the session or revoke refresh tokens in
+the POST handler **before** returning success, and expire the actual cookie with
+its original Domain/Path attributes. Browser cleanup alone cannot revoke a copied
+token or a session held by a separate authentication service.
+
+## Validation
+
+`npm run build` performs syntax checks. `npm test` exercises the pure HTTP handler
+with in-memory requests/responses and audits policy compatibility of the markup.
+Neither command opens a listening socket or imports `server.js`. No browser or
+dev/preview server was used for this change.
+
+References: [CSP deployment](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Practical_implementation_guides/CSP),
+[Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data),
+[Referrer-Policy and Origin](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Referrer-Policy),
+[Trusted Types enforcement](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/require-trusted-types-for).
