@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# SMS verification integration
+
+This repository is a static frontend. SMS delivery, code validation and authenticated sessions require a same-origin backend; the frontend does not simulate successful authentication.
+
+## API contract
+
+Both endpoints accept JSON via POST and return JSON. Use HTTPS in production.
+
+- `/api/auth/sms/request`: accepts `{ "phone": "+15551234567", "purpose": "rewards" }` (or `"order"`). Normalize and validate the phone server-side. Return `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id", "maskedPhone": "+1 ••• ••• 4567" }` only after accepting SMS delivery. Resend uses this endpoint again and must invalidate earlier codes for that number.
+- `/api/auth/sms/verify`: accepts `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-issued-id", "code": "012345", "purpose": "rewards" }`. Check the code server-side and consume the challenge atomically. Establish a secure HttpOnly session cookie and return `{ "verified": true }` only on success. The browser then navigates to `/rewards` or `/order-online`; these destinations must also enforce authentication.
+- Return 400/401 for invalid input or codes, 410 for expired challenges, and 429 for rate limits. Other failures receive a generic unavailable message. No phone numbers or codes are stored in browser storage.
+
+Generate six-digit cryptographically random codes, preserve leading zeros, expire them promptly (e.g. five minutes), limit verification attempts, and enforce SMS delivery limits server-side. The UI's 30-second resend cooldown is only a convenience. Bind challenges to a same-site session and enforce Origin/CSRF checks for both endpoints. Never use the client-provided purpose as an authorization decision.
+
+## Required SMS format
+
+Start with readable text and end with the actual deployed hostname (no scheme, path or port), a single space, and `#` followed by the code. Nothing may follow the last line:
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind verification code is 012345. It expires in 5 minutes.
+
+@your-deployed-hostname.com #012345
+```
+
+The frontend feature-detects WebOTP in secure contexts and starts listening before requesting delivery. It aborts requests on submission, resend, number changes, timeout and page exit. Unsupported or declined WebOTP falls back to OS code autofill and manual entry through the same accessible input.
