diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c747efb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+A static page; no build step is required. Run `npm test` for the validation tests.
+
+## Integration configuration
+
+`validation.js` assumes the trusted ordering service is `https://orders.dailygrind.coffee`
+and the privately controlled site domain is `dailygrind.coffee`. Change both constants
+to the deployment's actual values. Never configure a public suffix or shared hosting
+domain as the private site domain.
+
+The ordering service can send `{ type: 'order-complete', message: '…' }` or
+`{ type: 'redirect', url: '…' }` via `postMessage`. Only the exact configured ordering
+origin is trusted; redirect destinations must additionally be same-site with the
+page. `?returnTo=…` exposes a Continue link only for a valid same-site destination.
+URLs must use HTTP(S) and have no embedded credentials. External HTTP(S) links
+remain navigable and receive `noopener noreferrer`; unsafe links are blocked.
+
+The local rewards iframe uses `sandbox="allow-scripts"`. Only its first `init`
+message can pin its identity; subsequent `widget-status` messages are accepted
+from that sender. Messages render as text, never HTML.
+
+When available, the browser's `Origin` API supplies schemeful, Public Suffix List
+aware site comparisons and preserves opaque sender identities. Without it, URL
+parsing supplies exact ordering-origin checks. Site validation allows subdomains
+only for the explicitly configured private domain; other deployments conservatively
+allow the same scheme and hostname. It never guesses a registrable domain.
+
+Without `Origin`, widget validation pins the expected iframe's `WindowProxy` and
+requires a serialized `null` origin. This rejects other sandboxed iframes but cannot
+distinguish navigation to another opaque document within that same iframe. With
+`Origin`, navigation creates a new identity and is rejected until the parent reloads.
+
+Tests run in Node without servers or a browser. Native `Origin` behavior is covered
+by a contract fixture; actual browser internals and rendering are not exercised.
