+# Daily Grind rewards
+
+Node 24+ and pnpm are required. Install with `pnpm install`, check with `pnpm build` and `pnpm test`, and run in production with `pnpm start`.
+
+## Configuration
+
+- `PORT`: listener port (11222 by default).
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: exact public HTTPS origin, e.g. `https://coffee.example.com`. Required for production: it is the cryptographically checked audience and allowed POST origin. Default is `http://localhost:11222` for local use. Terminate HTTPS at your reverse proxy.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: a valid Chrome Email Verification origin-trial token registered for that exact origin. Served as the `Origin-Trial` response header. Register/renew at https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification. Without trial enablement, browsers will generally submit no token.
+- `DATABASE_PATH`: SQLite file (default `./data/rewards.sqlite`), on a persistent volume. Back up and restrict access to this directory. Session challenges and accounts persist across restarts. All processes must share this database; separate replicas require a shared transactional datastore.
+- `EVP_ISSUER_HOSTS`: comma-separated operator-approved issuer hosts, default `accounts.google.com`.
+- `EVP_JWKS_HOSTS`: comma-separated operator-approved JWKS hosts, default `accounts.google.com,www.googleapis.com`. Add hosts only after reviewing provider documentation. Token-supplied hosts outside these lists are never fetched. DNS email-domain delegation is still mandatory.
+
+The start command now serves the dynamic homepage and `POST /api/verify-email`; a static file host cannot provide registration. No dev or preview server is needed to build or test.
+
+## Registration and limitations
+
+The native HTML form carries `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` with a fresh, server-rendered nonce. Native submission is intentional: Chrome populates the hidden token during form submission. No JavaScript intercepts or reads the token. The browser must support Email Verification, and the customer must be signed in to a participating email provider in the same browser profile. Gmail is a participating provider in the origin trial.
+
+The server consumes the session nonce atomically and validates the submitted email, DNS delegation, issuer metadata, issuer signature, holder signature, nonce, audience, freshness, and SD-JWT binding with `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`. Accounts are stored only after successful verification; passwords are salted and scrypt-hashed. Verification is registration proof, not an authentication session or proof of inbox deliverability. Account login and redemption are outside this change.
+
+Per the no-email requirement, unsupported browsers, missing tokens, cancellations and invalid proofs produce an actionable retry message and never send a passcode or magic link. They do not create an unverified account. Failed submissions receive fresh challenges and preserve name/email, but never preserve passwords. The session lifetime is ten minutes; each POST consumes its challenge even if invalid.
+
+Before public deployment, configure the origin and trial token, persistent storage, HTTPS, and reverse-proxy request/rate limits. The server enforces a 32 KiB request limit. Origin checking plus a session-bound CSRF field protect submission. Token contents and passwords are not logged.
+
+Tests use in-memory SQLite and call the application handler directly; they do not start listeners or import `server.mjs`. Cryptographic tests use generated signing keys without network calls.
