+
+## Browser agent tools
+
+The shared header exposes WebMCP tools on every page. In a browser supporting
+WebMCP, agents can invoke `open_search` or `open_reading_list` to reveal the
+corresponding declarative form tools:
+
+- `search_articles`: search titles and summaries; returns matching stories and URLs.
+- `manage_reading_list`: view, save, or remove an article in the current session.
+  Saving an already saved article or removing an absent article leaves the list as is.
+
+Both forms use the same submit handlers for people and agents. Agent submissions
+receive JSON through `SubmitEvent.respondWith`; normal submissions update the UI.
+`get_article` is also available throughout the site and returns article metadata,
+plus the article body when the requested story is currently open.
+
+Article pages additionally expose their existing data utilities:
+
+| Article | Tool |
+| --- | --- |
+| `super-bowl-lx` | `get_article_popularity` |
+| `coastal-pulse` | `get_tide_levels` |
+| `coffee-alchemy` | `get_coffee_brew` |
+| `silent-architects` | `get_plant_stats` |
+
+These tools report status, final URL, redirect information, and response data,
+including the intentional demo service failures. Requests honor agent cancellation.
+The React integration scopes registrations to component lifetimes and aborts them
+on unmount, including Astro client navigation. Browsers without WebMCP retain the
+ordinary forms and controls; no polyfill or external MCP server is needed.
+
+WebMCP is experimental and requires a supporting browser and a secure context.
+See the [WebMCP specification](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/).
